Third-party software in grpc-asr-server
---------------------------------------

grpc-asr-server, the one program this repository builds, links only
permissively licensed code:

- whisper.cpp and ggml (https://github.com/ggml-org/whisper.cpp, v1.9.3,
  fetched at build time): MIT License.
- miniaudio (third_party/miniaudio/miniaudio.h, vendored): public domain
  (Unlicense) or MIT No Attribution, at your choice.
- gRPC (https://github.com/grpc/grpc, v1.83.0, fetched at build time):
  Apache License 2.0, together with the libraries it builds from its own
  third_party tree (Protocol Buffers, Abseil, BoringSSL, c-ares, RE2, upb,
  zlib), each under the permissive license recorded there.

FFmpeg in the container images (GPL)
------------------------------------

The published images (CUDA, CPU and OpenVINO) also contain the ffmpeg and
ffprobe executables, installed from the Ubuntu archive of each image's base
(ubuntu:26.04 for the CPU and OpenVINO images, which ships FFmpeg 8.0;
ubuntu:24.04 under the CUDA runtime image, which ships FFmpeg 6.1).

Ubuntu configures that FFmpeg with --enable-gpl and links GPL components
into it (libx264, libx265, libxvid and others), so these two
executables are licensed under the GNU General Public License, version 2
or later, as a whole. Their copyright file is /usr/share/doc/ffmpeg/copyright
in the image.

grpc-asr-server does not link FFmpeg. It starts ffmpeg and ffprobe as
separate processes (video containers and Ogg audio) and exchanges data with
them only through a memfd and pipes. The server binary therefore carries no
GPL code, but each image does: anyone redistributing an image redistributes
these GPL executables and takes on the GPL's obligations for them,
including making their corresponding source available. That source is
Ubuntu's ffmpeg source package at the version installed in the image
(`dpkg -s ffmpeg` prints it; `apt-get source ffmpeg=<version>` fetches it).

Every image states this in its ai.pipestream.ffmpeg.license label and
carries this file at /usr/share/doc/grpc-asr/NOTICE. Like any Ubuntu-based
image, the images also contain the base system's own packages under their
own licenses, recorded under /usr/share/doc.
